---
import ActionButton from '../../components/ActionButton/ActionButton.astro'
import CalloutBlock from '../../components/CalloutBlock/CalloutBlock.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock/CodeBlock.astro'
import CompareTable from '../../components/CompareTable/CompareTable.astro'
import LineageCard from '../../components/LineageCard/LineageCard.astro'
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead/SectionHead.astro'
import RootLayout from '../../layouts/RootLayout.astro'
import { SITE } from '../../lib/site'
import './LineagePage.css'

const ANCESTORS = [
  {
    year: '2008',
    name: 'OOCSS',
    author: 'Nicole Sullivan',
    body: [
      'The first serious argument that CSS needed object-oriented thinking. Two rules: separate structure from skin, and separate container from content. A component should not care where it sits, and a shape should not carry a color.',
      'It was right, and it was twenty years early. The tooling to enforce it did not exist, so it survived as folklore rather than as a system.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Separate structure from skin – this became <strong>Mass vs Void</strong>.',
      'A component never knows where it lives, which is the Binary Rule with fewer words.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'The class-level mixing (<code>.media.img-left</code>) that made markup a shopping list.',
      'Nothing enforceable. OOCSS was advice; BEAM is a checklist.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2009',
    name: 'BEM',
    author: 'Yandex',
    body: [
      'The most important idea in the history of CSS naming, and the direct parent of this project. BEM said: give every component a namespace, name its parts explicitly, and never write a descendant selector again. Twenty-odd years later that is still correct.',
      'What went wrong was ergonomics. <code>.block__element--modifier</code> costs you four extra characters per name and a mental parse on every read. Then Sass gave everyone <code>&amp;__element</code>, and the class strings in your HTML stopped existing anywhere in your stylesheet.',
      'BEAM is BEM with the tax removed and the two missing pieces added.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'The entire mental model: block, part, no descendant selectors.',
      'Explicit relationships over implicit nesting.',
      'One namespace per component.',
    ],
    drops: [
      '<code>__</code> and <code>--</code>. One hyphen carries the relationship; underscores separate words.',
      'Modifiers as classes. Variation is data, so it belongs in an attribute.',
      '<code>&amp;__</code> concatenation, which deletes your class names from your own codebase.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2011',
    name: 'SMACSS',
    author: 'Jonathan Snook',
    body: [
      'Categorize before you write: base, layout, module, state, theme. The insight was that not all CSS is the same <em>kind</em> of CSS, and that mixing kinds in one file is why stylesheets become unnavigable.',
      'SMACSS asked you to hold the category in your head. BEAM puts it in the class name, where you cannot forget it.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Categories as a first-class concept – they became the <code>l_</code>, <code>u_</code>, <code>g_</code> prefixes.',
      'Layout as a separate category from components.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'State as a category of <em>class</em> (<code>.is-active</code>). State is an attribute.',
      'Theme as a layer of overrides. Theming is a pointer swap, not a cascade of exceptions.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2014',
    name: 'ITCSS',
    author: 'Harry Roberts',
    body: [
      'Order the entire stylesheet by reach and specificity: settings, tools, generic, elements, objects, components, utilities. An inverted triangle where specificity only ever increases. Genuinely brilliant, and it made large legacy codebases tractable.',
      'It also required you to maintain a mental specificity graph forever. BEAM reaches the same outcome differently: flat single-class selectors have nothing to escalate against, IDs are forbidden as styling hooks, and <code>:where()</code> pins resets to zero.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Ordered global layers – the global stylesheets are ITCSS with the arguing removed.',
      'Resets must not compete with components.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'The specificity graph as a thing humans maintain by hand.',
      'IDs as a styling hook. Specificity <code>1,0,0</code> is how the inverted triangle explodes.',
      'Seven layers you assemble yourself. BEAM ships six filenames.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2014',
    name: 'CSS-in-JS',
    author: 'styled-components, Emotion, and friends',
    body: [
      'The correct diagnosis: styles need scoping, and they should live next to the component that owns them. The prescription was to move CSS into JavaScript, and that is where the bill arrived – a runtime, hashed class names, styles in your JS bundle, and theme values delivered through a React context.',
      'Open devtools on a CSS-in-JS app and you are looking at <code>hXlkjP</code>. You cannot search for it, an agent cannot reason about it, and neither can you in six months.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Co-location. One block, one file, next to the component – that is the <strong>M</strong>.',
      'Scoping as a non-negotiable.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'The runtime. A stylesheet should not require JavaScript to exist.',
      'Generated class names. Names are for humans and for grep.',
      'Themes through a component context, when the cascade already does this natively.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2017',
    name: 'Utility-first',
    author: 'Tailwind CSS, and Atomic CSS before it',
    body: [
      'The most successful CSS methodology ever shipped, and it earned that. Its real contribution was not short class names – it was proving that <strong>a constrained scale beats unlimited freedom</strong>. Given eight spacing steps instead of infinity, designs got more consistent, not less.',
      'The cost is that appearance moved into the markup. Every color is declared twice, once per theme. Every instance of a component repeats every decision. And the class attribute stops describing what a thing is, which is precisely the information a reader needs most.',
      'BEAM takes the constraint and puts it back where it can be named: in tokens.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Constrained scales for space, type, radius and color.',
      'Design tokens as the single source of truth.',
      'The insistence that you should not be inventing values by hand.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'Appearance in the class attribute. Identity goes there instead.',
      'Per-instance repetition of every visual decision.',
      '<code>dark:</code> variants, which are duplicated theme definitions by another name.',
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2020',
    name: 'CUBE CSS',
    author: 'Andy Bell',
    body: [
      'Composition, Utility, Block, Exception. CUBE argued that layout should be <em>composed</em> from a small set of primitives rather than enumerated per component, and it is the reason the <code>l_*</code> primitives exist in this document.',
      'CUBE also reached for <code>data-*</code> attributes to express exceptions, which is the same instinct that became the <strong>A</strong> in BEAM. Where the two part company is strictness: CUBE is a philosophy you interpret, and BEAM is a specification you comply with.',
    ],
    keeps: [
      'Composition over enumeration – <code>.l_stack</code>, <code>.l_cluster</code>, <code>.l_grid</code>, <code>.l_switcher</code>.',
      'Attributes as a styling hook.',
      'Global utilities kept deliberately tiny.',
    ],
    drops: [
      'Optional blocks. Every visual thing gets an identity, always.',
      'Interpretation. Ambiguity is how conventions die.',
    ],
  },
]

const MATRIX_COLUMNS = ['BEM', 'Utility-first', 'CSS-in-JS', 'CUBE', 'BEAM']

const MATRIX_ROWS = [
  {
    concern: 'What the class says',
    cells: [
      { text: 'What it is', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'What it looks like', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'A hash', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'What it is', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'What it is', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'State',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Modifier class', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Conditional class strings', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Props and JS logic', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Attributes, by convention', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'Attributes, by rule', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Theming',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Up to you', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Every color declared twice', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'JS theme context', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Custom properties', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'Declared twice in total', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Add a third theme',
    cells: [
      { text: 'New override sheet', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Touch every component', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'New theme object', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'New token block', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'One new block, zero components', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Find a class by name',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Broken by <code>&amp;__</code>', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Nothing to search for', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Impossible', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Yes', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'Yes, by design', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Runtime cost',
    cells: [
      { text: 'None', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'None', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'JS runtime, per render', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'None', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'None', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Build step',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Optional. Sass, usually', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Required', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Required', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Optional', verdict: 'good' as const },
      {
        text: 'Optional – only for <code>fluid()</code>',
        verdict: 'good' as const,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Markup readability',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Good, if verbose', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'Spaghetti', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Clean, until devtools', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Good', verdict: 'good' as const },
      { text: 'One class per concern', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Reviewable by rule',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Partly', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'By linter, not by eye', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'No', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'By taste', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Every rule mechanical', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
  {
    concern: 'Agent-friendly',
    cells: [
      { text: 'Names guessable, nesting is not', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Verbose, drifts between files', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'No ground truth at all', verdict: 'bad' as const },
      { text: 'Needs interpretation', verdict: 'mixed' as const },
      { text: 'Name derived from filename', verdict: 'good' as const },
    ],
  },
]

const description =
  'BEAM CSS in context: what OOCSS, BEM, SMACSS, ITCSS, CSS-in-JS, utility-first CSS and CUBE each got right, what they cost, and exactly which parts BEAM keeps and drops.'
---

<RootLayout title="Lineage – BEAM CSS" description={description}>
  <div class="lineage_page">
    <div class="l_container" data-size="page">
      <header class="lineage_page-masthead">
        <div class="l_stack" data-gap="5" data-align="start">
          <span class="g_tag" data-tone="accent">v{SITE.version}</span>
          <h1 class="lineage_page-title u_balance">Standing on the shoulders of giants.</h1>
          <p class="lineage_page-lede u_pretty">
            BEAM is not a rejection of what came before it. It is a reading of eighteen years of CSS methodology,
            keeping the parts that held up and refusing the parts that turned out to cost more than they returned. Here
            are the receipts, one ancestor at a time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="l_stack" data-gap="0">
        {ANCESTORS.map((ancestor) => <LineageCard {...ancestor} />)}
      </div>

      {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- MATRIX */}
      <section class="lineage_page-section" id="matrix">
        <div class="l_stack" data-gap="fluid-md">
          <SectionHead
            eyebrow="Scorecard"
            title="The comparison, without the marketing."
            lede="Every column here has shipped real products at real scale. This is about trade-offs, not winners – but the trade-offs are not symmetric."
          />

          <CompareTable
            caption="How five CSS approaches handle nine concerns"
            columns={MATRIX_COLUMNS}
            rows={MATRIX_ROWS}
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* ----------------------------------------------------- WHAT IS NEW */}
      <section class="lineage_page-section" id="new">
        <div class="l_stack" data-gap="fluid-md">
          <SectionHead
            eyebrow="Net new"
            title="Three things, and only three."
            lede="If almost everything is borrowed, it is fair to ask what is actually being proposed. Here is the honest inventory."
          />

          <div class="l_stack" data-gap="8">
            <div class="lineage_page-claim">
              <div class="l_switcher" data-threshold="4xl" data-gap="6" data-align="start">
                <div class="lineage_page-claim_head">
                  <span class="lineage_page-claim_index">01</span>
                  <h3 class="lineage_page-claim_title">State is data, without exception</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="g_prose lineage_page-claim_body">
                  <p>
                    CUBE reached for attributes. Others have flirted with them. BEAM makes it absolute: there is no such
                    thing as a state class, so there is nothing to decide and nothing to relitigate. That absoluteness
                    is what turns a preference into something a linter – or an agent – can enforce.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lineage_page-claim">
              <div class="l_switcher" data-threshold="4xl" data-gap="6" data-align="start">
                <div class="lineage_page-claim_head">
                  <span class="lineage_page-claim_index">02</span>
                  <h3 class="lineage_page-claim_title">The double-mapped theme engine</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="g_prose lineage_page-claim_body">
                  <p>
                    Everyone uses custom properties for theming. Almost nobody separates <em>what a color is</em> from
                    <em>what a context means</em> from <em>what a component asks for</em>. Three layers instead of one
                    is the difference between “theming is possible” and “a color is declared exactly twice in the entire
                    codebase.”
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    It also produces the contextual inverse for free, which is the trick nobody has because nobody
                    separated the layers.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lineage_page-claim">
              <div class="l_switcher" data-threshold="4xl" data-gap="6" data-align="start">
                <div class="lineage_page-claim_head">
                  <span class="lineage_page-claim_index">03</span>
                  <h3 class="lineage_page-claim_title">Naming optimized for the cursor</h3>
                </div>
                <div class="g_prose lineage_page-claim_body">
                  <p>
                    Every previous convention optimized for reading. BEAM optimizes for <em>editing</em>:
                    <code>snake_case</code> so one double-click takes the whole token, a single hyphen so the relationship
                    is unambiguous, and an outright ban on building names with the ampersand so that every class in your HTML
                    exists as a real string in your repository.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    It sounds like a micro-optimization. It is the difference between a codebase you can refactor with
                    confidence and one you can only add to.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* ------------------------------------------------------- MIGRATION */}
      <section class="lineage_page-section" id="migration">
        <div class="l_stack" data-gap="fluid-md">
          <SectionHead
            eyebrow="Getting here"
            title="Migration, per starting point."
            lede="You do not convert a codebase. You convert the next component you were going to touch anyway, and then the one after that."
          />

          <div class="l_grid" data-cols="auto" data-min="lg" data-gap="4">
            <CodeBlock
              file="From BEM"
              lang="css"
              code={`/* Before */
.promo-card { }
.promo-card__title { }
.promo-card--featured { }

/* After: mostly mechanical */
.promo_card { }
.promo_card-title { }
.promo_card[data-featured='true'] { }`}
            />
            <CodeBlock
              file="From utility-first"
              lang="html"
              code={`<!-- Before -->
<div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
     <article class="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-xl
  border bg-white p-6 dark:bg-stone-900">

<!-- After -->
<div class="l_stack" data-gap="3">
  <article class="promo_card">`}
            />
          </div>

          <CalloutBlock tone="note" title="BEAM coexists">
            <p>
              Nothing about BEAM requires deleting your current stylesheet. The global files are additive, the
              <code>l_*</code> primitives do not collide with utility names, and a BEAM component sitting next to a legacy
              one is fine. Convert on contact.
            </p>
          </CalloutBlock>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* --------------------------------------------------------- WHEN NOT */}
      <section class="lineage_page-section" id="when-not">
        <div class="l_stack" data-gap="fluid-md">
          <SectionHead
            eyebrow="Counter-indications"
            title="When not to use this."
            lede="A methodology that claims to fit every situation is selling something. Here is where BEAM is the wrong answer."
          />

          <div class="l_grid" data-cols="auto" data-min="lg" data-gap="4">
            <div class="lineage_page-caveat">
              <h3 class="lineage_page-caveat_title">A happy, disciplined utility team</h3>
              <p class="lineage_page-caveat_body">
                If you already run utilities with a real token config, extracted component classes and a linter that
                bites, you have solved most of the same problems by another route. The switching cost is real. Take the
                theme engine and leave the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="lineage_page-caveat">
              <h3 class="lineage_page-caveat_title">HTML email</h3>
              <p class="lineage_page-caveat_body">
                Custom properties, nesting and container queries in Outlook. Go with God. Use tables and inline styles
                like the rest of us, and do not tell anyone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="lineage_page-caveat">
              <h3 class="lineage_page-caveat_title">A team that will not agree</h3>
              <p class="lineage_page-caveat_body">
                BEAM's value comes from being absolute. Half-adopted, it is just another naming scheme with exceptions,
                which is worse than the convention you have. Get agreement first, or do not start.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="l_cluster" data-gap="3">
            <ActionButton href="/spec/">Read the specification</ActionButton>
            <ActionButton href="/install/" tone="outline">Install it</ActionButton>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</RootLayout>
